Before the measles vaccine (1963), the US reported 3–4 million cases per year; by 2000 measles was declared eliminated in the US.
The mechanism is well understood: vaccines train the immune system to recognise a pathogen so it responds faster and stronger on real exposure.
Herd immunity is mathematical reality — once ~95% of a population is immune to measles, transmission chains break and even unvaccinated people are protected.
Countries that delayed COVID vaccination rollouts (due to supply issues) had dramatically higher death rates than those with early high coverage, providing a natural experiment.
No alternative intervention — not vitamin supplements, not prior mild infection alone — provides the same breadth or durability of protection as vaccination.
